# CodeWashGame

CodeWashGame is a React game built with Vite and packaged for desktop with Electron.

## Development commands

Run these commands from the project directory:

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `npm run dev` | Start the Vite development server |
| `npm run build` | Create the production web build in `dist/` |
| `npm run preview` | Preview the production web build |
| `npm run electron:dev` | Build and launch the game in Electron |
| `npm run electron:build` | Build the portable Electron application |
| `npm run lint` | Run ESLint on `src/` |
| `npm run test` | Run Vitest tests |
| `npm run test:coverage` | Run Vitest with coverage thresholds |

## Performance improvements

> Mission: improve startup and in-game responsiveness while preserving gameplay, animations, and transitions.

### **Issue:** [#12](https://github.com/EpitechBachelorPromo2028/B-ING-300-PAR-3-1-codewash-21/issues/12) — Loading screen and startup responsiveness [P2] [Open]

- **Status:** The Loading screen and profiling performance are done. It remains Optimizations, Measurement and Tests.
- **Observed problem:** The issue reports a sluggish main menu and asks for visible startup progress. Before the change, there was no dedicated loading screen or asset preload progress, and the React interface rendered directly.
- **Impact:** Players had no visible indication of startup progress. The issue also asks for improved responsiveness, which needs to be confirmed through profiling and measurement.
- **Risk:** Preloading can delay access to the menu if too many assets are required. A loading screen improves feedback while loading, but does not by itself prove that startup is faster.
- **Fix:** Show a full-screen loading view, preload assets needed by the initial game screen, reveal the game when the preload finishes, and use profiling to guide further optimization.

#### Implementation details

- The loading screen uses a percentage, a bordered progress bar, and a short opacity transition to the game.
- At startup, a new `So... So fast !?` achievement reports the time taken to load the game in seconds.
- The preload covers the selected theme background, logo and card-back image, all six skill icons, mob sprites rendered on the initial screen, and eight small UI sound effects warmed in the background.
- Failed critical images are reported with a Continue action. Failed optional sounds do not block access to the game.
- Background music and combat sprites are not startup-critical and are not preloaded. The six background music WAV files total about 201 MB.

| File | Change |
| --- | --- |
| `src/components/LoadingScreen.jsx` | Adds the loading screen and accessible progress indicator. |
| `src/components/LoadingGate.jsx` | Coordinates preload state and provides the elapsed load time to the startup achievement. |
| `src/utils/preloadStartupAssets.js` | Loads critical images and warms optional UI sounds. |
| `src/constants/achievements.js`, `src/components/ui/AchievementToast.jsx`, `src/App.jsx` | Define and display the startup achievement, including the measured time. |
| `src/main.jsx` | Mounts the loading gate at the entry point. |
| `src/index.css` | Adds responsive red-screen styling and reduced-motion handling. |
| `src/utils/soundManager.js` | Aligns UI and mob sound URLs with files present in `public/assets/sounds`. |

---

### **Issue:** [#12](https://github.com/EpitechBachelorPromo2028/B-ING-300-PAR-3-1-codewash-21/issues/12) — Performance profiling and optimizations [P2] [Open]

- **Status:** React profiling and the selected optimizations are implemented and manually checked. The game interactions and deferred image loading were verified. TTI and production before/after measurements remain outstanding.
- **Observed problem:** Before the change, updates from the game caused the mounted Settings, Achievements, and Cosmetics drawers to render even while closed. Their Settings content also rendered three `ProfileCard` instances.
- **Player impact:** These renders add React work during interactions. The captures do not establish that players experienced a visible delay, so no user-visible slowdown is claimed.
- **Behavior before:** The drawers stayed mounted to preserve their slide transitions, and each parent update rendered their contents.

#### Optimizations

- **Observed problem:** App updates also rerendered closed drawers, display-only components, and images outside the visible area. Global styles were embedded in a JSX component, and all drawers and modals were in the startup import graph.
- **Impact:** These patterns can add rendering work and load code or images before the player needs them. No production speedup is claimed without a controlled before/after measurement.
- **Behavior before:** Drawers and modals were statically imported, global styles were injected by `GlobalStyles.jsx`, and images had no default lazy-loading behavior.
- **Fix:** Memoize display-only components and closed drawers; load drawers and modals with `React.lazy` on first use; extract global CSS and load it under the loading screen; lazy-load images and limit startup sprite preloading to the visible viewport.

| File | Change |
| --- | --- |
| `src/components/drawers/SettingsDrawer.jsx` | Skips parent updates while closed and preserves slide transitions. |
| `src/components/drawers/MenuDrawer.jsx` | Skips parent updates while closed and uses current props when opened. |
| `src/components/drawers/CosmeticsDrawer.jsx` | Skips parent updates while closed and uses current props when opened. |
| `src/App.jsx` | Loads drawers and modals with `React.lazy` and `Suspense` when first needed. |
| `src/components/ui/GlobalStyles.css`, `src/components/LoadingGate.jsx` | Extracts global styles and loads them during the loading screen before the game is revealed. |
| `src/components/ui/SafeImage.jsx`, `src/components/PhantomEvent.jsx`, `src/utils/preloadStartupAssets.js` | Defers off-screen images and preloads only sprites visible in the initial viewport. |
| `src/components/ui/SafeImage.jsx`, `src/components/ui/PixelHeart.jsx`, `src/components/ui/MobWithAura.jsx` | Memoizes display components to skip renders when their props have not changed. |

- **Code splitting per skill type:** Considered. `SkillCard` currently owns state, effects, and challenge handlers shared across the six mini-games. Splitting the skill implementations would require extracting that state and interaction logic; it was not included in this change because it would be a broader gameplay refactor without a profile showing that this code is a startup bottleneck.

#### Performance profiling

#### Measurement method and results

- Used React DevTools Profiler with the Vite development server at `http://localhost:5173/`; the exported profile identifies `LoadingGate` as the root and Vite source modules. Captures were made around the Skip / Manual Success interaction.
- The earlier profile recorded 10 React commits with a median of 79.35 ms, maximum 277.2 ms; `MenuDrawer`, `CosmeticsDrawer`, `SettingsDrawer`, and their profile cards appeared in 8 commits.
- The fresh profile `scripts/profil Skip.json` recorded 13 commits with a median of 85.0 ms, maximum 142.3 ms after two Skip / Manual Success presses. It contains no render-duration entries for the three drawers or `ProfileCard`; `App` and `SkillCard` still render.
- These commit durations are development-profiler render measurements, not end-to-end input latency.

#### Baseline metrics

- **FCP:** 522.9 ms, one Opera GX measurement on the production preview (`http://localhost:4173/`).
